# Best-effort packages — install-pkg.sh installs these in one xbps
# transaction (one at a time only if it fails); a single missing or renamed
# package is skipped and the run carries on.
#
# THE CRITERION: applications and conveniences. Losing one costs you that
# program and nothing else — no daemon stops starting, no keybind silently
# stops working across the board, and the theming engine keeps running.
# Anything whose absence quietly breaks a shipped feature belongs in
# packages/desktop.lst instead; anything the installer's own next step needs
# belongs in packages/core.lst.
#
# Failures here are still reported: install-pkg.sh names every skipped package
# in its closing summary regardless of tier. The tier decides how loudly, not
# whether.
#
# Being an application is NOT on its own a reason to be here. `thunar` and
# `firefox` are applications and live in desktop.lst, because each backs a
# keybind that silently does nothing without them. The test is the criterion
# above — does its absence stay quiet? — not what kind of program it is.
#
# Format: one package name per line. '#' starts a comment (whole-line or
# trailing). Blank lines ignored.
#
# NOT LISTED HERE, deliberately:
#
#   JetBrains Mono (plain or Nerd Font) — Void packages neither. The Nerd
#     Font glyph face is the vendored Cascadia Code NF (scope I, D3).
#
#   ddcutil / i2c-dev — brightness is xrandr gamma on this desktop target,
#     not DDC/CI. See the roster Epic's locked decision 10.
#
#   brightnessctl — it drives /sys/class/backlight, which a desktop GPU does
#     not expose. Since scope-h (2026-10-13) it is in packages/laptop.lst,
#     installed on laptops only, and dwm-brightness prefers it whenever a
#     backlight device exists.
#
#   power-profiles-daemon — laptops only, so it is in packages/laptop.lst
#     (scope-h, 2026-10-13).
#
#   unrar — Void's nonfree repo. `unar` below extracts RAR from main, so the
#     archive path needs no nonfree package. Locked decision 8.
#
#   p7zip — Void has it, but `7zip` below is upstream 7-Zip and covers it.
#
#   vim — the minimal build; `vim-huge` below is the full editor `vim.dcol`
#     themes (the old Fedora name was vim-enhanced).
#
#   Every name in this file is checked against a snapshot of Void's repo
#   index by tests/void-pkgnames.sh. Void's names are case-sensitive
#   (`Thunar`, not `thunar`).
#
#   The X11 and font -devel headers, plus gcc/make/patch/pkgconf —
#     all moved to packages/build.lst, which install-suckless.sh installs
#     itself immediately before it compiles.

# core system & display server
# xsetroot, opendoas and fuse are referenced by nothing in config/ or
# scripts/ (verified 2026-08-08). Kept as the diagnostic and privilege tools
# a bare Void base install lacks, not because anything here calls them.
# (xinput was one of them until 2026-10-22: dwm-shotkey needs it, desktop.lst.)
xsetroot
xsel
network-manager-applet
opendoas
fuse
alsa-pipewire
libjack-pipewire

# virtual machines
# spice-vdagent resizes the X screen to the viewer window and shares the
# clipboard with the host. autostart.sh starts its session agent only when
# the session's VM check says this IS a VM; on real hardware it sits
# installed and unused. Best-effort tier: a missing package only means a VM
# keeps a fixed resolution, which announces itself the moment you look.
spice-vdagent

# fonts & theming
# The Noto faces fonts.conf names (Sans, Serif, Sans Mono, Arabic) are all in
# noto-fonts-ttf, in desktop.lst. jetbrains-mono-fonts-all used to sit here
# as a spare coding face; Void does not package it and nothing named it.
#
# Deliberately NOT listed: gtk-murrine-engine. It is a GTK2-only engine,
# and themes/dark/theme.conf selects Adwaita-dark, which is pure CSS (a GTK4
# built-in, and a vendored one-line shim for GTK3) and never loads it. Add it only alongside an actual Murrine-based GTK2 theme.
noto-fonts-emoji
papirus-icon-theme
adwaita-icon-theme
# Desktop portal. config/xdg-desktop-portal/portals.conf names the gtk
# backend explicitly — a dwm session sets no XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP, so without
# it gtk is reached only as a warned-about last-resort fallback.
# It serves the GTK file chooser and, through its Settings portal, the
# 'prefer dark' colour scheme that libadwaita apps, Firefox, Chromium and
# Electron read. That scheme lives in dconf (the gtk backend reads it from
# GSettings and nowhere else), and install-restore-apps.sh writes it there
# through dbus-run-session when the install runs with no session bus. The
# dbus package itself is in desktop.lst (decision D2 needs the system bus).
xdg-desktop-portal-gtk
dconf
# Flatpak, with the Flathub remote added --user by install-restore-flatpak.sh
# (an explicit rule-4 exception, like the nonfree repository) and four read-only
# global overrides so sandboxed apps see the vendored cursor, the icon theme
# and the wallpaper gtk.css. Dark mode reaches them through the portal above.
flatpak

# desktop utilities & file management
# pavucontrol is the GUI mixer the volume block's click action opens; pamixer
# (desktop.lst) is what the keys themselves drive.
pavucontrol
alsa-utils
# Full palette tool — schemes, mixing, variations. Strictly optional: the
# keybound path is config/dwm/bin/dwm-colorpicker (Super+c), which samples one
# pixel with maim + ImageMagick and needs no GUI. gpick is here for the times
# you want to actually build a palette rather than read one colour.
#
# NOTE: ROADMAP §3 named `xcolor` for this row. Fedora had no such package,
# so the picker became a script. Void does package xcolor (0.5.1_2); the
# script stays because it works and needs nothing new.
gpick
# The clock's month calendar (left-click; config/dwm/bin/dwm-calendar) is a
# GTK 3 script, and this is its binding. Without it the click falls back to
# the month as a notification. Chosen over yad, which on Void pulls in
# libwebkit2gtk41 (122 MB) for one popup (scope K, 2026-10-27). The region
# screenshot's frozen-frame selector (dwm-shotselect) needs it too; without
# it region shots fall back to slop, which cannot catch an open dropdown.
python3-gobject
# Hides the mouse pointer after a few idle seconds; it comes back on the
# next move. autostart.sh starts it. The XFixes rewrite (binary: unclutter),
# not the older `unclutter` package of the same binary name.
unclutter-xfixes
# bluetooth: bluez is the stack, blueman the GTK manager the statusbar
# block's click action opens (sub-task 7).
bluez
blueman
# File-manager add-ons (roster Epic sub-task 8). `Thunar` itself is in
# desktop.lst — it backs super + e and is the mime handler for directories —
# and everything below only enriches it: thunar-archive-plugin + file-roller
# handle archives (unar covers RAR from Void's main repo — locked decision 8,
# unrar is in nonfree); thunar-volman + gvfs auto-mount removable
# media; tumbler + ffmpegthumbnailer produce video/PDF thumbnails; catfish
# is the search front end. Losing any one of these degrades Thunar; losing
# Thunar takes the keybind with it, which is the tier difference.
thunar-archive-plugin
thunar-volman
tumbler
ffmpegthumbnailer
file-roller
unar
catfish
gvfs
# xfce4-settings is here for ONE binary: /usr/bin/xfce4-mime-helper. Thunar
# hardcodes `exo-open --launch TerminalEmulator` for its built-in "Open
# Terminal Here", and exo 4.15.1 moved the helper framework out of exo
# ("Removed binaries: exo-compose-mail, exo-helper-2") into this package,
# which also ships the /usr/share/xfce4/helpers/ definitions that
# config/xfce4/helpers.rc names — alacritty.desktop among them. Without it
# helpers.rc is inert and that menu entry falls back to xfce4-terminal,
# which this roster does not install. Nothing starts xfsettingsd; the rest
# of the package is inert on a dwm session.
xfce4-settings
# desktop-file-utils ships update-desktop-database, which install-restore-apps.sh
# and uninstall-apps.sh both call — each behind a command -v guard with a
# yellow fallback, so a miss is announced rather than silent.
desktop-file-utils
lxappearance
xdg-user-dirs
unzip
7zip
zip
tar

# default applications (config/mimeapps.list). mpv is the video and audio
# player; its HEVC/HDR codecs come with Void's ffmpeg6 (desktop.lst), no
# third-party repo needed. nsxiv is the image viewer: Void's package is
# upstream v34 with the default config, the build suckless/nsxiv used to
# vendor before 2026-10-19 (Fedora did not package it). sublime-text4, the
# default editor, is in Void's nonfree repo and is installed by the repos
# step once nonfree is enabled (scope I, sub-task I-4), so it cannot be a
# line here.
mpv
nsxiv

# development stack (Node.js is handled by nvm, not xbps — see install-void.sh header)
neovim
vim-huge
tmux
# The tmux-256color terminfo entry (tmux's default-terminal). Void's ncurses-base
# lacks it; without it zsh inside tmux cannot clear a line and redraws leave junk.
ncurses-term
rust
cargo
python3-pip
fzf
bat
eza
zoxide
fastfetch
starship
htop
tree
pv
figlet
trash-cli
ripgrep
fd
curl
wget
binutils
coreutils
